mode: "100644"
text: "# Research, Types & Branded Tags\n\nHow to choose tags that do their (modest) job well, and how to build branded/campaign hashtags —\nthe one place hashtags still pull real weight.\n\n## The eight hashtag types\n\nMix types deliberately; each does a different job:\n\n- **Topic** — what the post is about (`#ProductManagement`). Core categorization.\n- **Niche/community** — a specific community you belong to (`#BuildInPublic`, `#BookTok`). Where\n  engaged people actually browse/search.\n- **Branded** — unique to you (`#MyCalvins`). Community + UGC. (See below.)\n- **Campaign** — tied to a launch/event (`#[Brand]LaunchWeek`). Tracking + participation.\n- **Product/service** — what you offer (`#SocialMediaScheduling`). Intent/search.\n- **Location** — geo, for local discovery (`#RotterdamStartups`). Powerful for local businesses.\n- **Format** — when the format is the value (`#Tutorial`, `#CaseStudy`). Use sparingly.\n- **Event/seasonal** — a live moment (`#SaaStrAnnual`). Time-bound only.\n\nA typical post uses a few across **topic + niche + (sometimes) branded/location** — not all eight.\n\n## Research & vetting\n\nChoosing relevant, mid-tier, safe tags matters more than the count:\n\n1. **Relevance first.** The tag must match the actual content. Irrelevant trending tags confuse the\n   algorithm and can hurt reach.\n2. **Aim mid-tier (~10K–500K posts).** Mega-tags (millions of posts) bury you in seconds; ultra-tiny\n   tags have no audience. Mid-tier = real demand you can compete in.\n3. **Check the tag page before using it.** Are the top posts like yours? Is there genuine\n   engagement? Is it overrun by bots/spam? If it looks spammy or off-topic, skip it.\n4. **Avoid banned/flagged tags.** Some innocuous-looking tags are spam-associated and suppress reach.\n   If a tag's page looks blocked or sketchy, don't use it.\n5. **Find them natively.** Use the platform's own search/suggestions and look at what well-performing\n   creators **in your niche** actually use (analyze, don't copy their list wholesale).\n\n## Build rotating sets, don't copy-paste one block\n\n- Build **2–4 reusable sets per pillar/topic**, then rotate by what each post is actually about.\n- Rotation keeps tags **relevant per post** (the thing that matters) and avoids the low-effort\n  copy-paste pattern.\n- Keep **1–2 consistent branded tags** across posts; vary the rest.\n\n## Branded & campaign hashtags (the durable use)\n\nThis is where hashtags still earn real value — community, UGC, and tracking:\n\n**Branded hashtag** (ongoing): a short, unique, memorable tag for your brand/community that followers\nuse when they post about you. Aggregates user-generated content and builds identity.\n\n**Campaign hashtag** (time-bound): tied to a specific launch/event/promo, for participation and\ntracking.\n\nDesign rules:\n- **Unique and unused** — search it first; make sure it isn't already taken or tied to something\n  off-brand.\n- **Short, easy to spell, hard to typo.** Read it out loud; check for accidental other-words.\n- **On-brand** and consistent across the campaign.\n- **Promote it** — put it in the bio, in captions, in the CTA (\"share yours with #__\"), so people\n  actually adopt it.\n- **Track it** via the platform's native search (WoopSocial has no hashtag analytics — see below).\n\n## Honest measurement\n\nWoopSocial has **no hashtag analytics surface**. To see what's working, the user pulls **native\ninsights** (e.g., Instagram's per-post \"reach from hashtags\") or reviews qualitatively. Treat\nhashtag choices as **experiments to observe**, not optimizations this skill can prove. Never claim\nperformance data you don't have.\n"
